  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major signaling pathways and drug targets in gastric or gastroesophageal cancer. It centers on the cell membrane, where several receptor tyrosine kinases—such as VEGFR, EGFR/HER1, HER2, c-MET, FGFR2, and IGFR—are shown with approximate frequencies of alteration or overexpression. Therapeutic antibodies and small-molecule inhibitors are mapped onto these receptors, indicating where agents like bevacizumab, ramucirumab, cetuximab, trastuzumab, and crizotinib act.\n\nInside the cell, the diagram highlights two main downstream cascades, the Ras/Raf/MEK/ERK and PI3K/PDK1/Akt/mTOR pathways, both feeding into nuclear programs that drive proliferation, survival, metastasis, and angiogenesis. Several inhibitors are placed along these pathways, including erlotinib, lapatinib, sorafenib, regorafenib, everolimus, and perifosine, emphasizing multiple intervention points.\n\nThe figure also includes additional mechanisms beyond receptor signaling, such as Hedgehog signaling through Ptch-1/SMO and GLI, proteasome activity, matrix metalloproteinases, histone deacetylase activity, and chromatin-remodeling genes. Overall, the image conveys that this cancer type is biologically heterogeneous and potentially treatable through many targeted strategies, each aimed at distinct molecular abnormalities and signaling nodes.",
